            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "mgl_material sets how surfaces answer light, for everything drawn after it.\n\nIT IS PUT BACK, LIKE A TRANSFORM:\nA material applies from where it sits onward and is restored at the end of its branch. A material placed before three shapes gives all three that material; a second material after them changes only what follows.\n\nBefore any mgl_material, the context's own material is in force: a dim grey ambient, white diffuse, a half-grey highlight and shininess 32 - the same as this node's defaults.\n\nHOW LIGHT, MATERIAL AND COLOUR COMBINE:\nShapes are always lit. What you see is roughly the light that reaches the surface (each light's ambient, plus its diffuse where the surface faces it, scaled by the material's ambient and diffuse) multiplied by the current colour, plus a highlight from each light's specular times the material's specular, which the colour does not tint. With no light at all, a shape shows a dim fifth of its colour. mgl_line_array and mgl_text are the exceptions: they draw in the current colour without any lighting.\n\nTHE COMPONENTS:\n'ambient' is the colour in shadow, 'diffuse' the colour in plain light, 'specular' the colour of the highlight, and 'shininess' how tight that highlight is. High shininess is a small hard glint; low is a broad sheen. Each one multiplies the matching component of every light.\n\nThe alpha of 'ambient' and 'diffuse' multiplies into the shape's transparency.\n\nCOLOURS:\nRed green blue alpha, from 0 to 1 or from 0 to 255. The scale is read from red, green and blue alone: if any of them is above 1, the whole colour, alpha included, is read as 0 to 255. The colour pickers store a full alpha as 255 whatever scale the colour is in, so an alpha above 1 on a 0 to 1 colour is read as 0 to 255 too - a patch opens again with the colours it was saved with.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmgl_material\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmgl_material\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmgl chain in:\nThe chain. This is what triggers the node.\n\nambient / diffuse / specular:\nHow the surface answers each part of the light, as red green blue alpha.\n\nshininess:\nFrom 1 to 256, 32 by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmgl chain out:\nThe chain, with this material in force downstream.\n\nRELATED:\nmgl_light for the light it answers; mgl_color for the hue. gl_material is the gl equivalent.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 810,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "mgl_material sets how surfaces answer light, for everything drawn after it.\n\nIT IS PUT BACK, LIKE A TRANSFORM:\nA material applies from where it sits onward and is restored at the end of its branch. A material placed before three shapes gives all three that material; a second material after them changes only what follows.\n\nBefore any mgl_material, the context's own material is in force: a dim grey ambient, white diffuse, a half-grey highlight and shininess 32 - the same as this node's defaults.\n\nHOW LIGHT, MATERIAL AND COLOUR COMBINE:\nShapes are always lit. What you see is roughly the light that reaches the surface (each light's ambient, plus its diffuse where the surface faces it, scaled by the material's ambient and diffuse) multiplied by the current colour, plus a highlight from each light's specular times the material's specular, which the colour does not tint. With no light at all, a shape shows a dim fifth of its colour. mgl_line_array and mgl_text are the exceptions: they draw in the current colour without any lighting.\n\nTHE COMPONENTS:\n'ambient' is the colour in shadow, 'diffuse' the colour in plain light, 'specular' the colour of the highlight, and 'shininess' how tight that highlight is. High shininess is a small hard glint; low is a broad sheen. Each one multiplies the matching component of every light.\n\nThe alpha of 'ambient' and 'diffuse' multiplies into the shape's transparency.\n\nCOLOURS:\nRed green blue alpha, from 0 to 1 or from 0 to 255. The scale is read from red, green and blue alone: if any of them is above 1, the whole colour, alpha included, is read as 0 to 255. The colour pickers store a full alpha as 255 whatever scale the colour is in, so an alpha above 1 on a 0 to 1 colour is read as 0 to 255 too - a patch opens again with the colours it was saved with.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmgl_material\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmgl_material\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmgl chain in:\nThe chain. This is what triggers the node.\n\nambient / diffuse / specular:\nHow the surface answers each part of the light, as red green blue alpha.\n\nshininess:\nFrom 1 to 256, 32 by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmgl chain out:\nThe chain, with this material in force downstream.\n\nRELATED:\nmgl_light for the light it answers; mgl_color for the hue. gl_material is the gl equivalent."
        },
